{"title":"Projective geometry","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Projective geometry is an area of geometry which focuses on properties that are invariant under projective transformations.\nUnlike Euclidean geometry, there is no notion of length; rather, any configuration of four collinear points or four concurrent lines is characterized by the cross-ratio.\nA particularly striking result of projective geometry is the duality of points and lines.\n</p><p>The first theorems of a projective character were established by the ancient Greeks, most notably Pappus of Alexandria.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Projective_Transformations\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Projective Transformations</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#The_Projective_Plane\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">The Projective Plane</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#The_Cross-Ratio\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">The Cross-Ratio</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Theorems\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Theorems</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Duality\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Duality</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Conics\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Conics</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Problems\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problems</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Further_Reading\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Further Reading</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Projective_Transformations\">Projective Transformations</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Projective_geometry&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Projective Transformations\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Given any two planes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> and a point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, we can define a projective correspondence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/9/8b90132a2854c90db9f20cdc6d3f14edcc0f5986.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(X): M \\rightarrow N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"121\" height=\"18\" /> as follows: for any point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/4/d94ac5fa84af4946a83c08f2de933d01b56e80c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(X)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"18\" /> is the intersection of line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/f/05fc2c59d25e97ec515446444b44cbe415d3f383.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PX$\" width=\"29\" height=\"12\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/4/d94ac5fa84af4946a83c08f2de933d01b56e80c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(X)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"18\" /> appears to be undefined when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/f/05fc2c59d25e97ec515446444b44cbe415d3f383.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PX$\" width=\"29\" height=\"12\" /> is parallel to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />. This issue will be addressed in the next section.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"The_Projective_Plane\">The Projective Plane</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Projective_geometry&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: The Projective Plane\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A key axiom of projective geometry is that any two lines meet in exactly one point, and through any two points there passes exactly one line.\nThe first part of this axiom breaks down in Euclidean geometry with parallel lines.\n</p><p>This problem is remedied by adding a \"point at infinity\" for each family of parallel lines.\nNow through any two points at infinity, there must be exactly one line passing through them, so we add a \"line at infinity.\"\nFinally, if we have a point at infinity and a regular point, the parallel postulate assures that there is exactly one line passing through both points.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"The_Cross-Ratio\">The Cross-Ratio</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Projective_geometry&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: The Cross-Ratio\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose we are given four points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/7/0a70ef3ff6592247a7bb65ba8bb54d724b112890.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C, D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"83\" height=\"18\" /> lying on a line.\nWe define the cross-ratio\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/0/6402c1d4a30b40ef2f2ec9ad6f988005dce4a22e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(A, B; C, D) = \\frac{(AC)(BD)}{(AD)(BC)},\\]\" width=\"212\" height=\"43\" />\nwhere directed lengths are used on the right side.\n</p><p><br />\nNow suppose we have four lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/c/42c06de735d79f0b28c30bcbbcf96e67bb1ca42c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, c, d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> passing through a point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and any fifth line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> not passing through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> be the intersection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> be the intersection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />, and so on.\nThen we define the cross-ratio of the four lines, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/0/fe044d646fce4c86629563a9e7df90651a9c2d7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a, b; c, d)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"71\" height=\"18\" />, as the cross-ratio of the four points, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/2/7928fbd1158fe433970a9329780a98aeec57c529.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(A, B; C, D)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"94\" height=\"18\" />.\nTo check that this cross-ratio is well-defined, note that by the area formula <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/b/97bc01566587eb5d8eacbf5e32c20fff51024c72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K = \\frac{1}{2}ab\\sin\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"108\" height=\"37\" /> we obtain\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/8/148c6371f670e0e46f8b80605e8dd7871777bc6b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} (A, B; C, D) &amp;= \\frac{(AC)(BD)}{(AD)(BC)} \\\\                   &amp;= \\frac{\\sin(\\angle{AOC})\\sin(\\angle{BOD})}{\\sin(\\angle{AOD})\\sin(\\angle{BOC})}, \\end{align*}\" width=\"315\" height=\"92\" />\nwhich is clearly independent of the transversal <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>The most important property of the cross-ratio is that it is preserved under projections.\n</p><p>Also, a set of four points or four lines are said to be harmonic if their cross-ratio is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/e/bae5aba07d37ff6ff813107e76260fb31ad5794e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" />. The complete quadrilateral illustrates the importance of harmonic points and lines:\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/6/13677fb64b50ffb05824b0ef8246d94eefd1d63d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EFGI$\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" /> be a quadrilateral. Sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/5/5f506b80246907627b4826e4d0fddd0a15f5913c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EI$\" width=\"24\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/5/3951902c11a1cd39a091ea15f0f06cbc79095de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> intersect at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, and sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/2/c128243ea8125d0bb007b8132dcfa44461df73a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/c/71c172fed92c9c7f0da03f85fad6f5445040d6de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$IG$\" width=\"23\" height=\"12\" /> intersect at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/7/927d4689159fbeb80f7b468ca9ae96b48e926312.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EG$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/8/6987ce01cfbd8256d13f69583906c05b4481cb8f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$IF$\" width=\"23\" height=\"12\" /> intersect line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, respectively, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/7/c3745bcb952c7eaea2dd1f10c49ed3cee1e95863.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(A, B; C, D) = -1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"146\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Theorems\">Theorems</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Projective_geometry&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Theorems\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>See <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pascal%27s_Theorem\" title=\"Pascal&#39;s Theorem\">Pascal's Theorem</a>, <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Brianchon%27s_Theorem\" title=\"Brianchon&#39;s Theorem\">Brianchon's Theorem</a>, <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Butterfly_Theorem\" title=\"Butterfly Theorem\">Butterfly Theorem</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Desargue%27s_Theorem\" title=\"Desargue&#39;s Theorem\">Desargue's Theorem</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Duality\">Duality</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Projective_geometry&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Duality\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Duality is the surprising principle that points and lines are interchangeable.\n</p><p>This principle arises from either the use of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Reciprocation&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Reciprocation (page does not exist)\">reciprocation</a> or the use of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Homogeneous_coordinates\" title=\"Homogeneous coordinates\">homogeneous coordinates</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Conics\">Conics</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Projective_geometry&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Conics\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Conic\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Conic\">conic</a> (or conic section) is a projection of a circle.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problems\">Problems</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Projective_geometry&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Problems\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The following problems are constructions involving only a straightedge (no compass).\n</p><p><br />\n1. Construct the fourth harmonic line to three given lines through a point.\n</p><p>2. Construct the fourth harmonic point to three points on a line.\n</p><p>3. If a given right angle and a given arbitrary angle have their vertex and one side in common, double the given arbitrary angle.\n</p><p>4. Draw a parallel through a given point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to two given parallel lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6ee61fd6e8a1926a802b97fc99b66b8687d8f620.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$l_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"11\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/6/1d69232050ab0ada641e7e2035f6e7c795795b65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$l_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"11\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Further_Reading\">Further Reading</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Projective_geometry&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Further Reading\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Coxeter, H.S.M. Projective Geometry.\n</p><p>Courant and Robbins. What is Mathematics?\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018101928\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.022 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.034 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 261/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 7986/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:9420:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018101928 and revision id 66999. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}